    "problem_statement": "# Sorting and slicing broken in PrettyTable\n\n## Description\n\nI've discovered an issue with the sorting functionality in PrettyTable. When using the `sort_key` parameter or combining sorting with slicing, the table doesn't display correctly.\n\n## How to Reproduce\n\nHere's a simple example that demonstrates the issue with `sort_key`:\n\n```python\nfrom prettytable import PrettyTable\n\n# Create a table with city data\ntable = PrettyTable(['City name', 'Area', 'Population', 'Annual Rainfall'])\ntable.add_row(['Adelaide', 1295, 1158259, 600.5])\ntable.add_row(['Brisbane', 5905, 1857594, 1146.4])\ntable.add_row(['Darwin', 112, 120900, 1714.7])\ntable.add_row(['Hobart', 1357, 205556, 619.5])\ntable.add_row(['Sydney', 2058, 4336374, 1214.8])\ntable.add_row(['Melbourne', 1566, 3806092, 646.9])\ntable.add_row(['Perth', 5386, 1554769, 869.4])\n\n# Try to sort by city name length\ndef key(vals):\n    vals[0] = len(vals[0])\n    return vals\n\ntable.sortby = 'City name'\ntable.sort_key = key\nprint(table)\n```\n\nThis should sort the cities by the length of their names, but it doesn't work correctly.\n\nSimilarly, when using sorting with slicing:\n\n```python\nfrom prettytable import PrettyTable\n\n# Create a table with numbers in reverse order\ntable = PrettyTable(['Foo'])\nfor i in range(20, 0, -1):\n    table.add_row([i])\n\n# This should show numbers 1-10, but doesn't work right\nprint(table.get_string(sortby='Foo', end=10))\n\n# The oldsortslice parameter also doesn't behave as expected\nprint(table.get_string(sortby='Foo', end=10, oldsortslice=True))\n```\n\n## Expected behavior\n\n- When using a custom sort key function, the table should sort according to that function\n- When combining sorting and slicing, the table should first sort all rows and then slice the result\n\n## Your project\n\nPrettyTable 3.8.0\n\n## Python version\n\n3.10.16"
  },
